    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_8f3d7647",
      "@type": "OverallIntent",
      "instance_name": "overallintent_instance",
      "label": "the overallintent",
      "description": "meme-id: meme_image; hasOverallIntent: Harmful; overall-intent-description: The meme uses a shocked woman image juxtaposed with text that falsely claims the Bible commands the death penalty for gay people, referencing Leviticus 20:13. This is a harmful misrepresentation of biblical text, as Leviticus 20:13 refers to ancient Israelite law and does not reflect modern Christian interpretations or the moral stance of most contemporary Christian denominations. The meme's intent is to provoke outrage or reinforce anti-LGBTQ+ religious rhetoric by misusing scripture, potentially causing harm to LGBTQ+ individuals and communities by promoting misinformation and religious condemnation. The image's exaggerated shock reaction is used to amplify the perceived 'shock value' of the false claim, making it more likely to be shared and believed, thus spreading harmful misinformation.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_5ebeb312"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_399bc734",
      "@type": "TextualMaterial",
