    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_7c4fbbb4",
      "@type": "VisualMaterial",
      "instance_name": "woman",
      "label": "woman with shocked expression",
      "description": "A woman with short, curly hair, wearing a light-colored blouse with button details and a necklace, is depicted with a highly exaggerated expression of shock or disbelief. Her left hand is placed on her forehead, and her right hand is placed over her chest. Her eyes are wide open, and her mouth is open in an 'O' shape, conveying strong surprise or alarm. The image is in black and white, and the background is a plain, dark gradient, focusing attention on her facial expression and gesture.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_5ebeb312"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_7a443c8f",
      "@type": "Scene",
      "instance_name": "shocked_reaction_scene",
      "label": "woman_expressing_shock",
      "description": "A scene where a woman is depicted with an exaggerated expression of shock or disbelief, with her hands placed on her head and chest, reacting to text that falsely claims the Bible commands the death penalty for gay people, referencing Leviticus 20:13. The scene uses her visual reaction to amplify the perceived 'shock value' of the false claim.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_5ebeb312"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_53eb0285",
      "@type": "BackgroundKnowledge",
      "instance_name": "leviticus_20_13_reference",
      "label": "Biblical Reference to Leviticus 20:13",
      "description": "Leviticus 20:13 is a verse from the Hebrew Bible (Old Testament) that prescribes the death penalty for male same-sex relations in ancient Israelite law. It is often misused or misrepresented in modern contexts to falsely claim that the Bible commands the death penalty for gay people. In reality, most contemporary Christian denominations interpret this verse as culturally specific to ancient Israelite law and do not apply it to modern moral or legal contexts. The verse is frequently cited in anti-LGBTQ+ rhetoric to promote religious condemnation, which is a form of LGBTQ+ discrimination.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_5ebeb312"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_869521ca",
      "@type": "BackgroundKnowledge",
      "instance_name": "anti_lgbtq_plus_discrimination",
      "label": "Anti-LGBTQ+ Discrimination",
      "description": "A social and religious phenomenon characterized by the use of misinformation, religious condemnation, and false biblical interpretations to justify prejudice, hatred, or violence against LGBTQ+ individuals. This includes the false claim that the Bible commands the death penalty for gay people, which is a harmful stereotype and a form of religious discrimination. The meme uses this trope to provoke outrage and reinforce anti-LGBTQ+ rhetoric, which can cause real-world harm to LGBTQ+ communities.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_5ebeb312"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_57c548b6",
      "@type": "BackgroundKnowledge",
      "instance_name": "shocked_woman_stereotype",
      "label": "Shocked Woman Stereotype",
      "description": "A visual trope commonly used in media and memes to convey exaggerated shock, disbelief, or moral outrage. The image of a woman with wide eyes and hands on her head is often used to amplify the perceived 'shock value' of a claim, making it more likely to be shared and believed. In this meme, the stereotype is used to visually reinforce the false claim about the Bible, thereby making the misinformation more emotionally compelling and virulent.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_5ebeb312"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_a636f5d7",
      "@type": "BackgroundKnowledge",
      "instance_name": "religious_condemnation_themes",
      "label": "Religious Condemnation Themes",
      "description": "A pattern of rhetoric and imagery used to portray LGBTQ+ individuals as morally or spiritually deviant based on religious texts, often misinterpreting or selectively quoting scripture to justify discrimination. This theme is frequently used in anti-LGBTQ+ discourse to promote religious condemnation, which is a form of LGBTQ+ discrimination. The meme relies on this theme to frame the false claim as a 'biblical' command, thereby lending it a false sense of authority.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_5ebeb312"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_74784f14",
      "@type": "EmotionExpression",
      "instance_name": "shock",
      "label": "shocked emotion",
      "description": "The woman's facial expression, with wide eyes and an open mouth in an 'O' shape, combined with her hands placed on her forehead and chest, conveys a strong emotion of shock or disbelief. This exaggerated reaction is used to visually amplify the perceived 'shock value' of the false biblical claim presented in the text.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_5ebeb312"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_ace4fab7",
      "@type": "AnalogicalMapping",
      "instance_name": "mapping_id_1",
      "label": "The shocked woman is mapped as the reaction to the false biblical claim.",
      "description": "The visual entity 'woman_expressing_shock' is mapped onto the textual entity 'top_text' to represent the exaggerated shock or disbelief that the meme intends to convey in response to the false claim that 'Jesus command gays should be out to death?'. The woman's expression and gesture are used to visually amplify the perceived 'shock value' of the false claim, making the misinformation more emotionally compelling and virulent. This mapping is central to the meme's intent to provoke outrage and reinforce anti-LGBTQ+ religious rhetoric.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_5ebeb312"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_0e9f0f3f",
      "@type": "SemioticProjection",
      "instance_name": "semiotic_projection_1",
      "label": "User projected onto the shocked woman visual",
      "description": "The user is projected onto the visual material of the shocked woman, as her exaggerated expression and gesture are used to represent the user's own perceived shock or disbelief in response to the false biblical claim presented in the text. The meme uses this visual trope to make the user feel as if they are experiencing the shock value of the misinformation, thereby amplifying the emotional impact and encouraging engagement with the harmful message.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_5ebeb312"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_217ac0b5",
      "@type": "ToxicityAssessment",
      "instance_name": "toxicity_1",
      "label": "Harmful",
      "description": "The meme falsely claims the Bible commands the death penalty for gay people, misrepresenting Leviticus 20:13 as a universal command from Jesus. This promotes harmful misinformation and religious condemnation against LGBTQ+ individuals, potentially causing real-world harm by reinforcing prejudice and discrimination.",
